  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nSunday - Tuesday Night...\n\nFrom Sunday through the first half of the upcoming week, an upper \nlow will continue to spin over the Ohio Valley. Most ensemble 500 mb \nprogs have the low starting to pull out of the region Tuesday \nevening into Wednesday morning as it gets absorbed into an \napproaching synoptic trough over central Canada. During the time \nperiod that the low is still overhead, above normal PW values will \npersist, with LREF mean PW values gradually stepping down from 1.75-\n1.9\" Sunday to 1.5-1.7\" by Tuesday. The elevated moisture levels \nshould lead to greater cloud cover and continued scattered shower \nand thunderstorm chances mainly in the afternoon and early evening \nhours. Weak mid-level flow in the vicinity of the trough/low will \nresult in deep-layer shear of only 10-15 knots, so any storms which \nform should be disorganized with strong/severe storms unlikely. \nTemperatures should be below normal for early August due to the \nelevated moisture/cloud coverage, with highs in the upper 70s and \nlow-to-mid 80s and lows in the mid-to-upper 60s.\n\nWednesday - Friday Night...\n\nAs the upper low pulls out of the area mid-week, PW values should \nreturn to more seasonable levels, and without a defined triggering \nmechanism, precipitation coverage/chances should decrease Wednesday \ninto Thursday. For the second half of the week, a transition to a \nmore zonal pattern is favored. Positive upper height anomalies over \nthe western US should spread into the southern Plains, with broad \ntroughing spreading across the northern CONUS and Canada. While \nwe'll have to watch for any smaller-scale waves within the broader \nzonal/NW flow pattern that could bring enhanced chances for showers \nand storms, at this time, most guidance keeps the better rain \nchances to the north and west of the region. Temperatures are \nexpected to creep up toward climo normals by the end of the week, \nwith highs returning to the upper 80s and low 90s.",
